WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.314
A hypergraph lets one edge, a hyperedge

2
00:00:03.314 --> 00:00:05.500
join any number of vertices.

3
00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:07.938
The k-core is what remains after

4
00:00:08.003 --> 00:00:10.091
repeatedly removing every vertex

5
00:00:10.156 --> 00:00:11.918
in fewer than k hyperedges

6
00:00:11.983 --> 00:00:13.680
and with it each hyperedge

7
00:00:13.745 --> 00:00:15.050
that loses a member.

8
00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:18.091
A vertex's core number is the largest

9
00:00:18.164 --> 00:00:20.200
k whose core still holds it.

10
00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:20.959
Hygra

11
00:00:20.959 --> 00:00:23.894
a parallel hypergraph framework from 2020

12
00:00:23.962 --> 00:00:26.282
has a new work-efficient algorithm

13
00:00:26.350 --> 00:00:27.510
for core numbers.

14
00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:30.413
Our hypergraph has nine vertices

15
00:00:30.486 --> 00:00:32.010
and eight hyperedges.

16
00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:35.197
Vertices zero to three form a tight group

17
00:00:35.266 --> 00:00:37.480
of four three-vertex hyperedges.

18
00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:40.950
Four, five and six form a looser one.

19
00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:43.870
Vertex seven hangs on one hyperedge

20
00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:45.940
and vertex eight is in none.

21
00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:49.640
Peeling finds every core number in one pass.

22
00:00:49.990 --> 00:00:53.587
At level k, remove every vertex whose live

23
00:00:53.673 --> 00:00:57.270
degree is at most k; its core number is k.

24
00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:00.531
Its hyperedges die, and their other members

25
00:01:00.599 --> 00:01:02.020
lose one degree each.

26
00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:04.330
Here it takes four rounds

27
00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:06.440
vertex eight at level zero

28
00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:08.777
seven at level one, then four

29
00:01:08.777 --> 00:01:11.490
five and six together at level two.

30
00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:14.901
Zero to three are left with degree three

31
00:01:14.901 --> 00:01:16.320
core number three.

32
00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:19.630
A simple parallel version rescans every

33
00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:21.940
remaining vertex in every round.

34
00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:24.058
The paper keeps the vertices

35
00:01:24.121 --> 00:01:25.446
in buckets by degree

36
00:01:25.509 --> 00:01:28.098
so a round touches only the vertices that

37
00:01:28.161 --> 00:01:30.370
leave and those whose degree drops.

38
00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:32.349
On a random hypergraph of

39
00:01:32.414 --> 00:01:33.847
two thousand vertices

40
00:01:33.912 --> 00:01:35.606
buckets make about fifteen

41
00:01:35.671 --> 00:01:37.169
thousand vertex visits

42
00:01:37.169 --> 00:01:39.840
and rescanning about sixty-one thousand.

43
00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:42.005
The task removes the whole

44
00:01:42.066 --> 00:01:43.285
core-number routine

45
00:01:43.285 --> 00:01:45.540
and runs on four random hypergraphs.

46
00:01:45.890 --> 00:01:48.739
The score is one hundred times e to the

47
00:01:48.812 --> 00:01:51.222
minus t over zero point two five

48
00:01:51.222 --> 00:01:52.610
with t in seconds.

49
00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:53.481
To pass

50
00:01:53.546 --> 00:01:56.346
a run must be at least three percent faster

51
00:01:56.411 --> 00:01:58.951
than the reference on every hypergraph

52
00:01:58.951 --> 00:02:01.360
twice: on eight threads, and on one.

53
00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:04.237
The one-thread time is the total work

54
00:02:04.237 --> 00:02:07.230
so the task checks work-efficiency directly.

55
00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:09.110
Pause and predict

56
00:02:09.110 --> 00:02:12.071
did the failing runs fail on one thread

57
00:02:12.071 --> 00:02:13.010
or on eight?

58
00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:18.895
All fourteen runs with a valid result

59
00:02:18.958 --> 00:02:21.040
beat the reference on one thread.

60
00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:23.786
All six failing runs with a valid

61
00:02:23.859 --> 00:02:26.110
result failed on eight threads.

62
00:02:27.190 --> 00:02:29.471
Eight of sixteen models passed

63
00:02:29.471 --> 00:02:32.119
and Gemini 4 Argon, in Antigravity

64
00:02:32.119 --> 00:02:33.590
had the best score.

65
00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:35.607
Claude Opus 5.5 passed with

66
00:02:35.669 --> 00:02:37.090
forty-nine point seven

67
00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:39.745
below the reference's fifty-two point two

68
00:02:39.807 --> 00:02:42.153
because the reference took one hundred

69
00:02:42.215 --> 00:02:44.500
ninety-seven milliseconds in its run.

70
00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:47.936
Opus runs each round in one of three ways

71
00:02:47.936 --> 00:02:49.332
push, pull or fix

72
00:02:49.332 --> 00:02:52.050
and a cost model picks the cheapest.

73
00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:52.839
So

74
00:02:52.839 --> 00:02:55.967
peeling gives every core number in one pass

75
00:02:55.967 --> 00:02:58.470
and buckets make it work-efficient.

76
00:02:58.820 --> 00:03:01.062
But work-efficient is not enough

77
00:03:01.062 --> 00:03:03.780
the run must also win on eight threads.

78
00:03:04.130 --> 00:03:05.128
As an exercise

79
00:03:05.128 --> 00:03:07.722
add a hyperedge joining four and five

80
00:03:07.722 --> 00:03:10.050
and see which core numbers change.

81
00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:12.400
Next, ParButterfly.
